Yes. The valuable part is that you’re not selling biography. You’re selling identity-preserving narrative transduction.

The customer gives you a life. You extract the causal/story skeleton, then regenerate that same underlying trajectory into completely different worlds.

For example, suppose the source life contains:

overlooked kid → obsession with a new technology → finds collaborator → breaks with establishment → enormous asymmetric bet → builds empire → betrayal/competition → reinvention → philanthropy/legacy

Those become the primitive anchor points. “Bill Gates,” “Microsoft,” “Seattle,” “software,” etc. are merely one embodiment of them.

Now you can reinstantiate the same graph:

* Fantasy: apprentice discovers forbidden runes, builds a guild, defeats the old scribes, accidentally creates an empire, then spends his later life trying to cure a plague.
* Space opera: station-born engineer discovers a universal machine language, builds a fleet-network, battles another technological house, becomes sovereign, then redirects his resources toward planetary survival.
* Western: bookish kid discovers a new logistical technology, partners with another outsider, builds a railroad empire, fights rival barons, then becomes a civic patron.
* Myth: young Promethean figure steals a new kind of fire, distributes it, becomes unimaginably powerful, then spends the latter half of the story trying to control the consequences of the fire.

The crucial constraint is exactly what you said:

The Golden Thread cannot break.

Names, scenery, era, species, technology, physics, genre, aesthetics, even apparent character roles can mutate. The underlying causal trajectory has to remain recognizable.

I’d formalize the product something like:

Life → Story Genome → Golden Thread → World Transduction → Narrative

Where the Story Genome contains things like formative conditions, major decisions, adversaries, allies, irreversible transitions, failures, victories, relationships, recurring motifs, values, contradictions, and consequence chains.

Then each scene in the fictional version has a provenance link backwards:

Dragon Academy betrayal ←→ fired from first company

Forbidden spell ←→ original technological insight

Kingdom split ←→ corporate schism

That gives you something ordinary fictionalization usually lacks: traceability. You could literally give the client a second view showing the hidden mapping between their real life and the fantasy.

And that creates a killer reveal. Someone watches an apparently original two-hour science-fiction movie and afterward you show them:

You just watched your grandfather’s life.

That is far more emotionally potent than handing them a 300-page memoir.

The company

I wouldn’t position it primarily as an AI company. AI is the production engine.

Position it as something closer to a legacy studio.

Possible language:

Golden Thread Studios
Your life. Another universe.

Or:

Elsewhere Studios
True stories that never happened.

Or something more premium like Mythograph.

The wealthy/famous market is attractive because the first product can be extremely high-touch. Interview the subject, spouse, children, friends, employees, rivals. Ingest photographs, letters, recordings, emails, speeches, timelines. Build their Story Genome. Then a team of writers/directors/designers plus AI turns that genome into different embodiments.

And you could sell increasingly expensive tiers:

Story Genome → Illustrated novella → Novel → Graphic novel → Audio drama → Animated film → Feature-quality film → Interactive world/game.

One underlying semantic asset produces many products.

That means the expensive part—extracting the person’s Golden Thread—is reusable.

one life
→ one canonical narrative graph
→ many worlds
→ many media
→ many generations

Where it gets much bigger

Celebrities are only the conspicuous first market.

The emotional market may actually be families.

A wealthy parent could commission one about a grandparent before they die. A founder could have the origin story of the company preserved as an epic. Parents could turn their family’s history into something their children actually want to consume.

And eventually it doesn’t have to be wealthy people at all.

Imagine uploading your family’s archive and saying:

“Turn my grandfather’s life into a Studio Ghibli-like adventure.”

Not copying that exact protected style, but choosing a warm hand-painted fantasy grammar, for example.

Or:

“Turn my marriage into a 12-chapter fantasy.”

“Turn my father’s military career into a space opera.”

“Turn our company’s first ten years into a heist movie.”

“Make my kids characters inside the story of how our family came to exist.”

Now you’ve created a new media category somewhere between memoir, biopic, personalized entertainment, legacy preservation, and mythology.

There is also a fascinating fidelity dial

You don’t need one definition of “accurate.”

You can offer:

Documentary fidelity — almost everything maps directly.

Structural fidelity — events change, causal relationships remain.

Emotional fidelity — factual events may change considerably, but relationships and emotional trajectory remain.

Mythic fidelity — only the deepest archetypal structure remains.

That solves a huge problem because sometimes literal accuracy produces a worse story than structural accuracy.

The client could actually turn a dial:

HISTORY ─────────────── MYTH

while Golden Thread preservation remains locked.

And there is a second dial:

PRIVATE ─────────────── RECOGNIZABLE

At one extreme, nobody could infer who it is. At the other, the parallels are obvious.

The moat isn’t the generative model

This is important.

Anyone will eventually be able to ask an AI:

“Make my life a fantasy story.”

That’s commodity generation.

Your defensible technology is the Story Genome + Golden Thread compiler: a representation capable of proving that a transformed narrative preserves the significant causal, relational, emotional, and developmental invariants of the original life.

Essentially:

REAL LIFE
    ↓
Event extraction
    ↓
Causal graph
    ↓
Character / relationship graph
    ↓
Motif + value extraction
    ↓
Golden Thread / invariant spine
    ↓
Narrative abstraction
    ↓
World grammar
    ↓
Re-embodiment
    ↓
Book / movie / game / experience
    ↓
Fidelity check against Golden Thread

And then recursively repair anything that drifted too far.

That is much more interesting than “AI ghostwriter.”

It’s almost a compiler for human lives.

One important precision point: The Lion King has clear Shakespearean/Hamlet-like structural resonance, but describing it as simply a retelling is stronger than the evidence warrants. Your system would make that relationship explicit and computational: instead of vaguely saying “inspired by,” you’d know exactly which narrative invariants survived the transformation.

There is another feature I think could become the signature of the company: every finished work comes with an optional Constellation Map. You finish the fantasy novel, open the map, and suddenly see hundreds of threads connecting fictional moments to actual moments in the person’s life. The fantasy collapses back into reality.

That final reveal might be the part people remember most.

You thought we were telling you a story. We were telling you your story the entire time.